A box in a vertical writing mode now exports its central baseline to a
parent it’s orthogonal to, unless its text is sideways. Blink’s
ComputedStyle::GetFontBaseline() picks the same: FontBaseline::kCentral
for vertical text that isn’t sideways, and kAlphabetic otherwise.

Run in Chrome 157.0.8103.79, Firefox 151.0, and WebKit 26.5 (the build
Playwright ships), all three give each row of vertical text in the test
(vertical-lr, vertical-rl, two lines, a nested block with a larger font,
upright, and sideways vertical-rl) the same offsets as Ladybird does
now, and the imported align-items-baseline-column-horz.html WPT test
passes all five of its subtests. Only sideways vertical-lr text still
differs: The engines put its alphabetic baseline on the descent side of
its line, and Ladybird puts it on the ascent side of that line.
